[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The assets worth attacking, who would attack them, how, the goals that answer
each threat, and the risk that remains.

These were five markdown tables in [`security.md`](security.md), promoted here
for the reason the architecture decisions and the hazards went to
[`04_software_arch.sdoc`](04_software_arch.sdoc) and [`safety.sdoc`](safety.sdoc): they
are the part of the concept that has identity. Forty-eight stable IDs with a
chain between them that was previously kept by hand in an appendix.

The chain is now relations the build checks:

- `Threatens` from each threat to the asset it is aimed at, and `Exploited by`
  to the adversary who can mount it,
- `Reaches` from a threat to the **safety hazard** it arrives at, which is the
  claim this document exists to make and the one that was hardest to see:
  [`HZ-12`](safety.sdoc) is a node now, so "a security compromise of this device
  is a safety event" is a link rather than a sentence,
- `Addresses` from each security goal to the threats it answers, and
  `Realised by` to the requirements behind it,
- `Remains under` from each residual risk to the goal that leaves it.

Two fields carry what the markdown carried as typography, and both are worth
more as fields.

**STATUS** replaces strikethrough. Four threats, two goals and one residual risk
were closed or dissolved by the read-only decision of 2026-10-06, and in the
markdown that was a `~~struck-through heading~~` plus a sentence. It is now
`Resolved` or `Withdrawn`, which can be filtered, counted and reported on. A
closed threat is kept rather than deleted: the reasoning for why it is closed is
the most useful thing about it, and a reader who finds the ID in an old commit
should land on an explanation.

**IMPLEMENTATION** replaces the `[built]` / `[partial]` / `[spec]` / `[absent]`
marks on the goals. `security.md` says the preponderance of `[spec]` is the
single most important fact in it; a typed field makes that countable instead of
a thing a reader has to notice.

Everything else stays in [`security.md`](security.md): the scope, the trust
boundary diagram, section 7 on security as a safety concern, what the
implementation already gets right, the obligations, the verification status and
the open questions. The trust boundaries `B-1` to `B-6` stay there too, and that
is a decision rather than an omission: they are the legend of the diagram
immediately above them, they carry no chain through them, and splitting a legend
from its picture helps nobody.

A reconciliation, stated because it changed what some relations say. The
appendix tables at the end of `security.md` were not updated by the 2026-10-06
read-only change, while sections 5, 6 and 9 were, so the two disagreed. Where
they disagree the body is taken as authoritative. The consequence is visible
and deliberate: with `SEC-01` and `SEC-04` withdrawn, the threats the stale
appendix routed through them ([LINK: TH-08] and [LINK: TH-11]) now have no
security goal at all, and so does [LINK: TH-13], which never had one. That is a
real gap, and naming it is better than inheriting a mapping to a goal that no
longer exists.
<<<

[[SECTION]]
UID: SECURITY-1
TITLE: 1. Assets

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
What is worth attacking, in the order an attacker would care about.
<<<

[ASSET]
UID: A-1
STATEMENT: >>>
**Control of the heater.**
<<<
WHY_IT_MATTERS: >>>
The kiln is a multi-kilowatt mains heater reaching 1350 °C. This is the asset; everything else is a means to it.
<<<

[ASSET]
UID: A-2
STATEMENT: >>>
**The safety configuration.**
<<<
WHY_IT_MATTERS: >>>
Maximum chamber temperature, runaway thresholds, current limits. Loosening these does not start a fire by itself, but it removes the layer that would stop one.
<<<

[ASSET]
UID: A-3
STATEMENT: >>>
**The latched-fault state.**
<<<
WHY_IT_MATTERS: >>>
A latched fault is the mechanism that keeps a failed kiln off ([LINK: SWR-SAF-17]). Clearing one remotely re-arms a kiln that something has already gone wrong with.
<<<

[ASSET]
UID: A-4
STATEMENT: >>>
**WiFi credentials.**
<<<
WHY_IT_MATTERS: >>>
`net.wifi_pass` is the user's network password, stored on the device. Its loss is a breach of their network, not merely of this device.
<<<

[ASSET]
UID: A-5
STATEMENT: >>>
**The web password.**
<<<
WHY_IT_MATTERS: >>>
`security.web_password`, guarding [LINK: A-1] to [LINK: A-3]. Removed from the configuration schema on 2026-10-06; the asset is kept because the threats against it are part of why it is gone.
<<<

[ASSET]
UID: A-6
STATEMENT: >>>
**The firmware image.**
<<<
WHY_IT_MATTERS: >>>
Write access to it is total and persistent control of [LINK: A-1].
<<<

[ASSET]
UID: A-7
STATEMENT: >>>
**Firing programs and run history.**
<<<
WHY_IT_MATTERS: >>>
Commercially meaningful to a production potter; the lowest-value asset here, and the only one whose loss is purely a privacy matter.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SECURITY-2
TITLE: 2. Adversaries

[ADVERSARY]
UID: ADV-1
STATEMENT: >>>
**Another device on the LAN**: a compromised IoT gadget, a guest laptop, malware on a household machine.
<<<
CAPABILITY: >>>
Full IP access to the device; no credentials.
<<<
REALISTIC: >>>
**Yes.** This is the normal case and the one [LINK: SYS-ASM-05] quietly assumes away.
<<<

[ADVERSARY]
UID: ADV-2
STATEMENT: >>>
**A malicious web page** the operator visits while on the same LAN.
<<<
CAPABILITY: >>>
Can make cross-origin requests from the operator's browser; can attempt DNS rebinding to defeat the same-origin policy.
<<<
REALISTIC: >>>
**Yes**, and it needs no LAN foothold at all.
<<<

[ADVERSARY]
UID: ADV-3
STATEMENT: >>>
**Someone with brief physical access**: a shared studio, a classroom, a workshop.
<<<
CAPABILITY: >>>
UART, JTAG, flash readout, reflash.
<<<
REALISTIC: >>>
**Yes**, given where kilns live.
<<<

[ADVERSARY]
UID: ADV-4
STATEMENT: >>>
**A later owner** of a second-hand or disposed device.
<<<
CAPABILITY: >>>
Everything in flash.
<<<
REALISTIC: >>>
**Yes**, and nobody thinks about it.
<<<

[ADVERSARY]
UID: ADV-5
STATEMENT: >>>
**An attacker from the internet.**
<<<
CAPABILITY: >>>
Only if the user port-forwards or the router is compromised.
<<<
REALISTIC: >>>
Only through a user error that the documentation must argue against.
<<<

[ADVERSARY]
UID: ADV-6
STATEMENT: >>>
**A supply-chain attacker** on a dependency.
<<<
CAPABILITY: >>>
Code execution in the image.
<<<
REALISTIC: >>>
Low: no package manager at build time, assets vendored ([LINK: UR-CON-04]), dependencies are ESP-IDF itself.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SECURITY-3
TITLE: 3. Threats

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Rated by consequence, not likelihood. A `Reaches` relation to a hazard means the
threat arrives at [`safety.sdoc`](safety.sdoc)'s analysis and therefore at
everything behind it; `SAFETY_RELEVANT` says so in a field as well, because the
distinction between "yes", "indirectly" and "closed" is the one a reader scans
for.
<<<

[THREAT]
UID: TH-01
STATEMENT: >>>
**Unauthenticated command execution.**
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Would have been direct control of the heater.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: Closed
STATUS: Resolved
COMMENT: >>>
**Closed by [LINK: SWR-WEB-26].** No network route can start a firing, abort one or command manual duty; the handlers are deleted, not disabled.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-1
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-12
  ROLE: Reaches
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-WEB-26
  ROLE: Closed by

[THREAT]
UID: TH-02
STATEMENT: >>>
**Loosening the safety configuration.**
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Would have removed the layer that stops a runaway rather than starting one.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: Closed
STATUS: Resolved
COMMENT: >>>
**Closed by [LINK: SWR-WEB-26].** `/api/config` is read-only; the maximum temperature and every safety threshold are writable only at the kiln. [LINK: SWR-SAF-22] and [LINK: SWR-SAF-23] bounded the damage even before that, which is the point section 7 of [`security.md`](security.md) makes about bounds doing security work.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-2
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-02
  ROLE: Reaches
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-10
  ROLE: Reaches
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-WEB-26
  ROLE: Closed by

[THREAT]
UID: TH-03
STATEMENT: >>>
**Clearing a latched fault remotely.**
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Would have re-armed a kiln that something had already gone wrong with.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: Closed
STATUS: Resolved
COMMENT: >>>
**Closed by [LINK: SWR-WEB-26].** Acknowledgement happens in front of the kiln. The fault stays readable, which is what the banner needs. [LINK: SWR-SAF-18] blunted it regardless: a fault cannot be cleared while its condition is still observably true, so an attacker could clear a stale fault but not a live one.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-3
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-08
  ROLE: Reaches
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-WEB-26
  ROLE: Closed by

[THREAT]
UID: TH-04
STATEMENT: >>>
**Hostile firmware reaching the device.**
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Total and persistent control of the heater.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: Yes
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
**Closed on 2026-10-06, reopened on 2026-10-09.** [LINK: SWR-UPD-01] used to close this threat absolutely, by accepting no image over the network at all. The Cyber Resilience Act requires that security fixes can reach a unit ([LINK: UR-REG-004]), `OQ-08` answered that with the pull path of [LINK: SWR-UPD-09] onwards, and a threat that is closed by the absence of a feature reopens when the feature arrives. Saying so is the point of keeping the node.

What stands in place of closure is no longer one property but three that have to hold together, and [LINK: SEC-12] is where they are stated: the manifest and the image verify against an **offline** key, nothing on the network can initiate or authorise an install ([LINK: SWR-UPD-08]), and the install waits for a person at the display ([LINK: SWR-UPD-12]). An attacker needs the key **and** somebody at the kiln pressing to confirm.

Kept as one threat rather than split into push and pull, because the asset and the consequence are unchanged and only the route is new.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-6
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-01
  ROLE: Reaches

[THREAT]
UID: TH-05
STATEMENT: >>>
**Cross-site request forgery and DNS rebinding.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
[LINK: ADV-2]; the operator's own browser is the confused deputy.
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Reduced by [LINK: TH-01] to [LINK: TH-03] being closed, and by [LINK: TH-04] having no inbound route to it: the worst a forged request can now do is **edit a stored firing program**, which takes effect only if an operator starts it at the kiln.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: Indirect
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-7
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ADV-2
  ROLE: Exploited by
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-12
  ROLE: Reaches

[THREAT]
UID: TH-06
STATEMENT: >>>
**Credential disclosure from flash.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
[LINK: ADV-3], [LINK: ADV-4], plaintext NVS, no flash encryption.
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
[LINK: A-4], the user's WiFi password, and [LINK: A-5].
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: No
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
Not a safety event, but it breaches the user's network rather than only this device.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-4
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-5
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ADV-3
  ROLE: Exploited by
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ADV-4
  ROLE: Exploited by

[THREAT]
UID: TH-07
STATEMENT: >>>
**Credential disclosure on the wire.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
No TLS; the password crosses the LAN in cleartext on every authenticated request.
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
[LINK: A-5], observable by [LINK: ADV-1].
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: No
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-5
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ADV-1
  ROLE: Exploited by

[THREAT]
UID: TH-08
STATEMENT: >>>
**Password brute force.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
Repeated requests against the auth check.
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
[LINK: A-5].
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: No
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
**Has no security goal.** It was answered by `SEC-04`, which was withdrawn on 2026-10-06 along with the password itself. Arguably it is moot rather than open, since there is nothing left to guess, but it is left Active and unanswered rather than quietly closed: if authentication ever returns, this threat returns with it, and it should be visible when that happens.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-5
  ROLE: Threatens

[THREAT]
UID: TH-09
STATEMENT: >>>
**Denial of service**: socket exhaustion, oversized bodies, slow clients.
<<<
VIA: >>>
Boundaries `B-2` and `B-4`; see section 3 of [`security.md`](security.md).
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Loss of the web interface. Crucially **not** loss of control: the safety supervisor is a separate task on the other core ([LINK: SWA-15]), and [LINK: SWR-NFR-02] bounds non-safety interference at 50 ms.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: No
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
Deliberately carries no `Threatens` relation. The interface is not one of the assets, and the asset an attacker would want, [LINK: A-1], is exactly the one this threat cannot reach.
<<<

[THREAT]
UID: TH-10
STATEMENT: >>>
**Information disclosure through unauthenticated reads.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
Every `GET` is unauthenticated by design.
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Telemetry, logs, programs, run history, network status ([LINK: A-7]).
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: No
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-7
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ADV-1
  ROLE: Exploited by

[THREAT]
UID: TH-11
STATEMENT: >>>
**Open provisioning access point.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
[LINK: SWR-NET-02] AP fallback; if `net.ap_pass` is empty the AP is open.
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Anyone in radio range reaches the provisioning page and can supply their own credentials.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: Yes
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
**Has no security goal.** The stale appendix routed it through `SEC-01`, which is withdrawn. This is the most security-sensitive surface the device has and the one most likely to be reachable without credentials, and it is currently answered by nothing but [LINK: OQ-S4]. Carried as [LINK: SRR-08].
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-4
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-12
  ROLE: Reaches

[THREAT]
UID: TH-12
STATEMENT: >>>
**Physical console access.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
UART console.
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
The simulator build exposes fault injection and direct commands.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: Yes
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
On a simulator build only: the console is compile-gated behind `CONFIG_KILN_PLANT_SIM` and is not in a production image. [LINK: SWR-NFR-24] is the requirement behind that.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-1
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ADV-3
  ROLE: Exploited by
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-12
  ROLE: Reaches

[THREAT]
UID: TH-13
STATEMENT: >>>
**mDNS and network reconnaissance.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
`kiln.local` advertised on the LAN ([LINK: SWR-NET-05]).
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Makes the device trivially discoverable; a precondition for the rest rather than a threat alone.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: No
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
**Has no security goal, and never had one.** Discoverability is accepted: the device is meant to be found by its operator, and `SWR-NET-05` asks for exactly that. Recorded so the acceptance is explicit rather than an omission.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ADV-1
  ROLE: Exploited by

[THREAT]
UID: TH-14
STATEMENT: >>>
**A hostile or impersonated update service.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
DNS spoofing or interception on the LAN ([LINK: ADV-1]), a compromise of `update.bitcrushtesting.com` or of the release process behind it ([LINK: ADV-6]), or an attacker on the path from the internet ([LINK: ADV-5]).
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
A manifest or an image of the attacker's choosing, which is [LINK: A-6] and therefore [LINK: A-1]. It fails unless they also hold the offline signing key; with the key, the last obstacle left is the person at the display.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: Yes
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
**This is the threat the update path buys, and it is why [LINK: SWR-UPD-11] refuses to lean on TLS.** Certificate validation is required and is not load-bearing: taking over the host, the DNS or the connection gets an attacker as far as *silence*, because what they serve has to verify against a key that is not on the server. Silence is its own problem, a fix that never arrives rather than a hostile one that does, and it is carried as [LINK: SRR-12] rather than pretended away here.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-6
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ADV-5
  ROLE: Exploited by
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-01
  ROLE: Reaches

[THREAT]
UID: TH-15
STATEMENT: >>>
**Downgrade to a release whose vulnerability is published.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
Replaying an older, genuinely signed manifest, or standing up a mirror and getting it into `update.url`.
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
A unit returned to a version with a known hole, after which the hole is the attack. The signature verifies, because the image is authentic; it is merely old.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: Indirect
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
Worth stating separately from [LINK: TH-14] because a signature does not stop a replay, and the usual reflex, "images are signed", does not answer it. [LINK: SWR-UPD-13] does: no image installs unless its version is **strictly greater** than the running one, and the service offers no route to a downgrade. The eFuse anti-rollback counter is deliberately **not** burnt, for the reason `security.md` section 6.2 gives: it would make the counter, rather than the operator, the thing that decides whether a unit can ever be recovered.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-6
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ADV-1
  ROLE: Exploited by

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SECURITY-4
TITLE: 4. Security goals

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
`IMPLEMENTATION` is what is actually true today, and the marks are not
decoration: most of this is **not built yet**.

- **Built**, implemented and covered by an automated test.
- **Partial**, implemented in the host-testable core, but the piece that would
  enforce it on a device does not exist.
- **Spec**, required or designed, no implementation at all.
- **Absent**, not required, not designed, not present. A gap, named as one.
- **NotNeeded**, the threat it would answer cannot occur.

At the time of writing there is **no HTTP transport on the target**. The REST API
is complete and host-tested, but the `esp_http_server` adapter that would
terminate TCP, parse headers, check a password and apply rate limiting is not
started. Nearly every network control here is therefore `Spec`, and that is the
single most important fact in this document.
<<<

[SECURITY_GOAL]
UID: SEC-00
STATEMENT: >>>
**No network request shall change any device state at all.**
<<<
IMPLEMENTATION: Built
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
Tested: every request other than `GET` returns `403 read_only`, program authoring included since 2026-10-06, and the handlers are **deleted from the image** rather than gated by a flag. This is the strongest control in this document, and the only one that does not depend on the unwritten HTTP transport, because it is enforced in the API layer that *is* written.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-01
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-02
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-03
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-04
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-05
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-WEB-26
  ROLE: Realised by

[SECURITY_GOAL]
UID: SEC-01
STATEMENT: >>>
Only an authenticated client may change state.
<<<
IMPLEMENTATION: Partial
STATUS: Withdrawn
COMMENT: >>>
**Dissolved 2026-10-06**: no client may change state, authenticated or not, so there is nothing left for authentication to gate. Kept rather than deleted because the route-selection half is still in the code and still worth not losing: `kiln_api_needs_auth` returns true for every non-`GET` method, so a route added later is protected *by default* rather than by somebody remembering to list it. Superseded in substance by [LINK: SEC-00].
<<<
