[[SECTION]]
UID: SECURITY-5
TITLE: 5. Security residual risk

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
What stands after the goals above, stated without softening. Each names who
carries it. There is no `SRR-10`: the numbering has a gap and keeping it is
cheaper than renumbering IDs that are cited from other documents.
<<<

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-01
STATEMENT: >>>
**There is no authentication on a device, because there is no HTTP server on a device.** Every network control is a specification.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
Milestone M6 is not implemented.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The project.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
None. It is not a *live* exposure, since an unimplemented server accepts no connections, but it must not be read as "the controls exist". **Nothing in this document may be cited as evidence of a deployed control.**
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-05
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-02
STATEMENT: >>>
The design and the data model disagree about the password.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
It does not. **Closed 2026-10-06**: `security.web_password` is removed from the configuration schema. There is no password, no hash, and nothing to disagree about.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
Nobody.
<<<
STATUS: Resolved
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-04
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-03
STATEMENT: >>>
**No TLS.** Every request crosses the LAN in cleartext, readable by [LINK: ADV-1].
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
A self-signed certificate on a `.local` name trains users to click through browser warnings, costs flash and RAM against a 2 MB OTA slot, and still cannot be verified. The honest trade was plaintext on a network already assumed trusted.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The owner of the network ([LINK: SYS-ASM-05]).
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
[LINK: SWR-NFR-20]'s "trusted LAN only", stated in the README, in [`safety.md`](safety.md) and here. It is a real limitation, not a solved problem.
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-11
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-04
STATEMENT: >>>
**Firmware images are validated but not authenticated, in the shipped firmware.** [LINK: SWR-UPD-03] requires checking an image is *valid for this target*. A requirement asking for a **signature** finally exists, [LINK: SWR-UPD-11] as of 2026-10-09, and secure boot has existed as a provisioning step since 2026-10-08; neither is implemented in the application, and a default build is neither signed nor provisioned.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
Signing is specified now and built nowhere. The secure boot mechanism is opt-in rather than default, so an unprovisioned board authenticates nothing, and [LINK: SWR-UPD-11]'s manifest and image verification is a requirement with no code behind it yet.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The project.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
Only [LINK: SWR-UPD-08]'s authentication stands between an attacker and [LINK: TH-04], and that is specification only. On an unprovisioned board, physical reflash ([LINK: ADV-3]) bypasses it entirely. [LINK: OQ-S2] is `Resolved` as of 2026-10-09 without closing this: a unit taken through `tools/secure-boot.py` will not run unsigned code, the supervisor has no equivalent and is deliberately not getting one, and no requirement asks for either. What is left for this risk is a `SWR-` for signing, not a mechanism.
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-05
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-05
STATEMENT: >>>
**Secrets are plaintext in flash.** No flash encryption. Anyone with the board reads the user's WiFi password.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
Not specified; flash encryption also complicates development and field recovery.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The owner, and anyone who disposes of a device.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
[LINK: SEC-14]'s factory reset, specified on 2026-10-09 as [LINK: SWR-CFG-09] and not yet built, which turns [LINK: ADV-4] from "a device sold on carries the credentials with it" into "a device sold on without being reset does". That is a real improvement and not a solution: the credentials are still plaintext while the device is in service, which is [LINK: OQ-S3] and is no longer a matter of taste, [LINK: UR-REG-004] asking for stored data to be protected by state-of-the-art means.
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-09
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-06
STATEMENT: >>>
**No CSRF defence.** A page the operator visits can issue cross-origin requests to `kiln.local`, and DNS rebinding defeats the same-origin policy.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
Not specified or designed. Currently harmless only because the interface is read-only, which is a property of [LINK: SEC-00] rather than of any CSRF work.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The project.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
None. Cheap to fix, since validating `Origin` and `Host` costs almost nothing, and it should land *with* the transport rather than after it.
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-10
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-07
STATEMENT: >>>
**All reads are unauthenticated**, and reads are now all there is.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
The interface is read-only by decision.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The design, deliberately.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
Defensible: the dashboard is meant to be glanceable and the data is low-value ([LINK: A-7]). But it is a decision, and it means the device is not private. Open as [LINK: OQ-S5].
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-07
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-08
STATEMENT: >>>
**The AP fallback may be open.** [LINK: SWR-NET-02] starts an access point with a provisioning page; nothing requires `net.ap_pass` to be non-empty.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
No minimum-passphrase rule exists.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The installer.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
None. The provisioning page is the most security-sensitive surface the device has, and it is the one most likely to be reachable without credentials. Open as [LINK: OQ-S4].
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-11
  ROLE: Remains against

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-09
STATEMENT: >>>
**[LINK: SYS-ASM-05] is the assumption everything rests on, and it is usually false.** Home and studio LANs routinely carry compromised IoT devices and guest traffic.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
A single-purpose controller cannot police its own network.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The owner.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
A VLAN or a dedicated network segment is the real answer; the documentation should say so rather than only saying "trusted".
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-11
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-11
STATEMENT: >>>
**No field update path, in a shipped image.** Removing OTA closed [LINK: TH-04] completely and left the device with no way to receive a fix, including a security fix, without physical access.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
**Specified 2026-10-09, built not at all.** `OQ-08` is resolved and [LINK: SWR-UPD-09] to [LINK: SWR-UPD-16] say what the path is, so what stands between a unit and a security fix is no longer a decision anybody still has to take. It is the HTTP client, the manifest verifier, the update screen and the rollback discipline, none of which exist, on a target that has no HTTP transport either ([LINK: SRR-01]).
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The project. **This blocks release**, and it blocks the SoldUnits route specifically, because [LINK: UR-REG-004]'s main obligations apply from 11 December 2027.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
[LINK: SWR-UPD-14]'s USB path, which is real today and needs a visit to every device. Nothing else, until the pull path is written.
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-04
  ROLE: Remains against

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-12
STATEMENT: >>>
**The update service is a dependency, and the signing key is the fleet.** Every unit that updates itself depends on `update.bitcrushtesting.com` resolving, answering and being honest, and on a key the project keeps. Lose the key and no unit can be updated again; lose the host, let the domain lapse or let somebody else acquire it, and the same is true with an additional hazard attached.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
Inherent in any pull update path. The alternative, no update path, is [LINK: SRR-11], and it is worse.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The project, for the lifetime of the support period, which outlasts most of the decisions in this document.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
The key is offline and is not the secure boot key; [LINK: SWR-UPD-11] means a compromise of the host alone yields silence rather than control; [LINK: SWR-UPD-12] means even a compromise of the key still needs somebody at the kiln; `update.url` lets a site mirror the service; and [LINK: SWR-UPD-14]'s USB path survives the service disappearing entirely. What has no technical mitigation is the domain and the key outliving the project's interest in them, which is an organisational commitment and should be written as one.
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-12
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-13
STATEMENT: >>>
**The check tells the manufacturer that a device exists, and needs the device to reach the internet.** A daily HTTPS GET discloses an IP address and a time to the party that sold the unit, and a device on a segment with no outbound route never checks at all.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
Any pull path reveals this much by existing. It is the price of not building a push path, which would have cost [LINK: SEC-00]'s absolute form instead.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The owner.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
[LINK: SWR-UPD-10] keeps the request free of anything about the device: static manifest, local comparison, no identifier, no version, no query. The check is disableable at the display and redirectable with `update.url`, so a site that will not let the controller out can mirror the manifest internally. Note the tension with [LINK: SRR-09], which advises an isolated segment: that advice and the update path now pull in opposite directions, and the documentation has to say which the owner should prefer and why, rather than printing both.
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-06
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-14
STATEMENT: >>>
**The Cyber Resilience Act's non-firmware obligations have no owner, no dates and no artefacts.** No published disclosure policy until 2026-10-09, no machine-readable SBOM, no procedure for the Article 14 reporting route, no declared support period, no Annex II information for users, and no decision on the conformity assessment route. None of it is code, and none of it is discharged by writing code.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
The project's instinct, and the reason its technical documentation is unusually good, is to answer a requirement by building something. Roughly half of what [LINK: UR-REG-004] requires cannot be built: it is a mailbox somebody reads, a file kept for five years, a date declared in a manual, and a report filed within 24 hours by a person who knows they have to.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The project, and specifically whoever signs the declaration of conformity. The **reporting** half is the sharp end: it applies from **11 September 2026**, more than a year before the main obligations of 11 December 2027, and it does not wait for the firmware.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
Two of the rows are closed as of 2026-10-09. `SECURITY.md` carries the disclosure policy, the single point of contact and the reporting commitments. The SBOM of [LINK: SWR-NFR-28] is a real build output, `tools/sbom.py`, generated from the component list the build writes and from the licence tags in each component's own sources, published in both CycloneDX and SPDX by the release workflow; what remains of that row is **retaining** the documents for the support period, which needs the period chosen.

Everything else stands. [LINK: SWR-UPD-16] requires the support period to be declared and nobody has declared it, and a requirement is not an artefact: `security.md` section 13 is the inventory, and most rows of its second table still need a name against them. The reporting procedure is the one with a date attached.
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-12
  ROLE: Remains under

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SECURITY-6
TITLE: 6. Open questions

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Each names the residual risk it would settle, so a question cannot drift away
from the risk that motivated it. Two were answered by the read-only decision of
2026-10-06 and are kept as `Resolved` rather than deleted, because the answer is
the useful part.
<<<

[OPEN_QUESTION]
UID: OQ-S1
STATEMENT: >>>
Salted hash or plaintext for the web password?
<<<
STATUS: Resolved
COMMENT: >>>
**Answered 2026-10-06 by removing the question.** The interface is read-only, so there is no password. Closed [LINK: SRR-02] with it.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SRR-02
  ROLE: Would settle

[OPEN_QUESTION]
UID: OQ-S2
STATEMENT: >>>
Should firmware images be **signed** ([LINK: SWR-UPD-03] requires validity, not authenticity), and should secure boot be enabled?
<<<
STATUS: Resolved
COMMENT: >>>
**Answered 2026-10-09, differently for each of the two microcontrollers of [LINK: SWA-22], with the argument in `security.md` section 6.1.** ESP32-S3: **yes**, signed images and Secure Boot V2, but as a per-unit provisioning step (`sdkconfig.secure`, `tools/secure-boot.py`) rather than a project default, because the field-recovery cost this question named is real and falls on self-builders: the key holder becomes the only party who can ever update a provisioned board. Supervisor (STM32G031K8T6): **no**, and there is nothing to enable, the G0 line having no ROM-verified signature chain and this part no key store or crypto accelerator; the readout protection it does have is refused on purpose, because RDP Level 2 is irreversible and would close the SWD that [LINK: SWA-22]'s independence argument depends on, while [LINK: ADV-3] can jumper the permit line more cheaply than reflash anything. Program-memory integrity is covered at run time by a CRC-32 over the whole image instead. This closes the question without closing [LINK: SRR-04], which stands while the default build is unsigned and no requirement asks otherwise. [LINK: OQ-08] is untouched, and now has a signing mechanism to use.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SRR-04
  ROLE: Would settle

[OPEN_QUESTION]
UID: OQ-S3
STATEMENT: >>>
Should flash encryption be enabled by default?
<<<
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
It complicates self-builders and field debugging, and it is the only answer to [LINK: SRR-05] while the device is in service.

**Reframed 2026-10-09, and the framing is the point.** This was recorded as a trade-off the project could decline, between a security property and the convenience of people who build their own boards. On the SoldUnits route it is not: [LINK: UR-REG-004] requires the confidentiality of stored data to be protected by state-of-the-art means, and the user's WiFi password sits in plaintext NVS. The two routes can diverge here, a self-build staying unencrypted and a sold unit not, in the same way [LINK: OQ-S2] let secure boot be a per-unit provisioning decision; what cannot happen is the question staying open while units ship. [LINK: SEC-14]'s factory reset reduces the disposal case and does nothing for a board lifted out of a working kiln.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SRR-05
  ROLE: Would settle

[OPEN_QUESTION]
UID: OQ-S4
STATEMENT: >>>
Should `net.ap_pass` have an enforced minimum, or should the access point refuse to start open?
<<<
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
[LINK: TH-11] currently has no security goal at all, so this question is the only thing standing against it.

**Promoted 2026-10-09 from an open question to the one on the critical path.** [LINK: UR-REG-004] requires a product to be shipped in a **secure default configuration**, and an access point that will start with no passphrase is a default-insecure state rather than a flexible one. Of everything the Cyber Resilience Act asks of this project, this is the only item that is a live weakness in code that exists today; the rest is either unbuilt or paperwork. Answering it is a few lines in the WiFi adapter and a bound in the configuration table.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SRR-08
  ROLE: Would settle

